{
  "name": "vector_light_invalidation_radius",
  "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
  "description": "DOES ONE WALL REBAKE THE MAP? The vector-lighting epic names MarkGeometryDirtyAround as the suspect that turns a single blocker write into a map-wide rebake, and until now nothing has actually asked it. The counters exist -- vector_light_invalidations, vector_light_invalidation_marks and their ratio vector_light_marks_per_call -- but the bake arms that carry them provoke no blocker writes at all, so the ratio reads 0 there and settles nothing. This scenario provokes the writes. \n\nTHE MEASUREMENT IS A RATIO, NOT A COUNT, which is why vector_light_emitters is pinned beside it in every arm. 'One wall marked two lights' means nothing without 'and there were 23 on the map'; the failure being tested for is marks_per_call rising towards the emitter count, and a run that quietly drifted to a three-lamp scene would report a healthy ratio while measuring nothing. \n\nTWO ARMS, BOTH PREDICTED FROM THE GEOMETRY BEFORE BEING MEASURED. A TorchLamp's glowRadius is 10 and MarkGeometryDirtyAround tests radius + 1, so a cell is marked by a lamp within 11. The scene's lamp grid is spaced 13 in x and 11 in z, which puts a great many cells exactly ON that boundary -- 11 squared against 11 squared -- so both arms use cells chosen for MARGIN rather than convenience: (25,18) is inside exactly one lamp's reach and 101 squared units clear of any other's boundary, and (24,9) is inside exactly two with 84 to spare. A cell picked by eye next to a lamp would have sat on the knife edge and turned this into a flake. \n\nTHE ARMS ARE SEPARATED BY vector_light_bake_reset, not by being separate runs: the counters accumulate, so arm two would otherwise report arm one's marks as well as its own. \n\nWHAT A FAILURE LOOKS LIKE. marks_per_call at or near 23 means the radius test is not discriminating and every wall really does dirty every light -- the thing the epic suspects. A marks count above the predicted one but far below 23 means the scene has emitters this file does not know about, which is a scenario bug rather than a code one: note that vector_light_emitters is 23 while the grid only places 20 lamps, so the save contributes three of its own, and where they sit is exactly what the pins record. \n\nvector_light_bakes is RECORDED rather than asserted. A mark is a promise to rebake and the rebake follows on the next frame, so the two numbers should agree -- but bakes also counts anything else the frame decided to rebuild, and pinning it would make this scenario fail for reasons that are not about invalidation. \n\nNO CIRCINUS, and no circinus_available pin either -- the first cut inherited one along with the setup steps it copied from vector_light_bake_cull, and duly failed a run whose fifteen real probes had all passed. Nothing here is timed; these are counters the field itself writes, so the profiler would only add a mod dependency and a clock the arms do not want moving. \n\nCLOUDS ARE OFF, and the scene, the lamp grid and the feature set are lifted verbatim from vector_light_bake_cull so the emitter population is the one the bake arms already measured.",
